        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>Air Leakage Areas:



Most of the air leakage is coming from the crawlspace. Utilitdor, Crawlspace Door, and floor below crawspace are very leaky areas. Sealing these areas properly will decrease air leakage into the home.



Attic hatch is leaky.



Bathroom fan is allowing cold air to enter the home when not in operation.



</airSeal>
                <ceilings>Vermiculite insulation in the ceiling should be addressed. May be a good idea to get this tested for asbestos. Do not disturb the insulation.



Add R-30 -40 of insulation to the attic.



Increase the R-Value of the sloped areas of the ceiling by having the attic cavities spray foamed. It will help with air sealing as well as increasing the insulation value.



Please refer to Artic Energy Alliance for rebates on insulation upgrades and the criteria for these products.</ceilings>
                <VentilationSystem>Replace exisitng bathroom fan with a new energy efficient model and a timer control for it. The damper should also be assessed as currently there is not one installed or it does not function properly.



If there are significant air sealing upgrades completed to the home, it will make it tighter, but it will not allow for the home to breathe as it was before. A mechanical ventilation system such as an HRV/ERV should be installed to allow the home to be properly ventilated. Not installing a system after air sealing can lead to issues with mold and mildew.



the HRV/ERV will recover heated exhaust air via a heat exchanger and preheat supply air with the extracted heat, making the units very efficient. Make sure the unit is ENERGY STAR® rated and HVI certified.



HRV = Heat Recovery Ventilator

ERV = Enthalpy Recovery Ventilator



ERV also can control indoor humidity levels as well.



Please refer to Artic Energy Alliance for rebates on ventilation systems and the criteria for these products.

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>The hot water storage tank is over 30 years old and should be replaced. 



I would recommend switching to a gas instantaneous on-demand hot water heater with minimum 0.92 EF (Energy Factor), or to a combi loop off of the boiler (at 0.79 EF). See Heating System Comments for more information about the combi boiler.



Please refer to Artic Energy Alliance for rebates on DHW systems and the criteria for these products.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>Upgrade the exisiting oil boiler to a Combi Gas Boiler. This would combine your heating and hot water into one system. Make sure to look for one that is ENERGY STAR® and has at least a 95% AFUE rating.



This system will have direct supply/exhaust and significantly decrease the amount of ice that is getting built up on the back side of the home. These units are condensing systems so they will remove moisture from the exhaust before it exits the home.



Install a digital or smart thermostat to help reduce fuel consumption.



Please refer to Artic Energy Alliance for rebates on Heating/DHW upgrades and the criteria for these products. </HeatingSystem>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
